🚀 Q2 — Find Numbers Greater Than a Given Value
🎗️ 2.1 Problem Statement

You are given a List<Integer> containing positive and negative integers and an integer threshold.
Using Java Streams, find all numbers that are strictly greater than the given threshold.

Your solution should:

👉 Use the Stream API.
🔴 Preserve the original encounter order.
🔴 Return the result as a List<Integer>.
🔴 Include only numbers strictly greater than the threshold.

🎗️ 2.2 Input / Output

👉 Input
numbers = [10, 25, 5, 40, 15, 30, 8]
threshold = 20

👉 Expected Output
[25, 40, 30]

🎗️ 2.4 Discussion of Your Solution
👉 Your solution:

List<Integer> list = integerList.stream()
        .filter(x -> x > threshold)
        .toList();

✅ Correctness
Your solution is correct.
For: [10, 25, 5, 40, 15, 30, 8]
threshold = 20

you get: [25, 40, 30]

👉 How it works

🔴 integerList.stream()
Creates a sequential Stream from the original list.

🔴 filter(x -> x > threshold)
Keeps only elements strictly greater than threshold.

🔴 .toList()
Collects the filtered elements into a List.

✅ Encounter Order

Your solution preserves the encounter order because you're using a sequential Stream over an ordered List.

Input: [10, 25, 5, 40, 15, 30, 8]
Output: [25, 40, 30]

The relative order of 25, 40, and 30 is preserved.

🎗️ 2.5 Time And Space Complexity

🔴 Time: O(N)

Every element may need to be examined.
N = number of elements
So: O(N)

🔴 Space: O(K)

Where K is the number of elements greater than the threshold.
Worst case: K = N
Therefore worst-case space is: O(N)
